; Sentora's own SELinux rules (AlmaLinux/Rocky with SELinux enforcing), loaded by the installer:
;   semodule -i sentora.cil
; Only what AlmaLinux's own policy does not cover and labels can not fix; each rule is as narrow as
; it can be. Nothing here switches anything off.

; BIND writes its log in Sentora's log folder (/var/sentora/logs/bind, labelled named_log_t): it may
; enter /var/sentora/logs (Apache's logs, httpd_log_t) on its way, but not list or read anything there.
(allow named_t httpd_log_t (dir (search)))

; The Sentora daemon (cron, every 5 minutes) runs postqueue (to count the mail queue) and sendmail (its
; alerts): they inherit the daemon's open files (its script, its report), which SELinux rightly closes
; for them. Nothing is allowed here: these two refusals are only not written to the audit log, as they
; are expected and change nothing (the mail queue is counted, the alerts are sent).
(dontaudit postfix_postqueue_t httpd_sys_content_t (file (read)))
(dontaudit system_mail_t httpd_log_t (file (write append)))

; The panel's log viewers (System Log Viewer, DNS Admin) ask whether they may read BIND's and the FTP
; server's logs, which SELinux keeps from the web server (they hold every client's names and files).
; The answer stays no; only the asking is not written to the audit log at every page.
(dontaudit httpd_t named_log_t (dir (read)))
(dontaudit httpd_t named_log_t (file (read open)))
(dontaudit httpd_t xferlog_t (dir (read)))
(dontaudit httpd_t xferlog_t (file (read open)))
